WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.840
Six mixed IMOCA crews start the first Ocean Race Atlantic from New York to Lorient on the 1st of September. This is Offshore North, Episode 3. Scott Shawyer is trying to become the first Canadian to finish the Vendée Globe, the solo nonstop round the world race that starts in 2028. Over to the Canadian boat, the campaigns website and YouTube channel were last updated on 17th August, with a full boat tour video and an article on the Canadian Offshore lineage. As of that date, the public channels

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:54.280
carried no new position reports after the boats departure from Cullingwood. Emira 4 already carries a proven record for the 2028 goal. It won the Ocean Race 2023 as Malama under Charlie N. Wright. Place 3rd in the 2024 to 2025 Vendée Globe with Sebastian Simon on one broken foil and set a solo 24-hour speed record with Simon before the Honda over in early 2025.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:01:20.320
Under Canada Ocean Racing, it finished 6th in the Ocean Race Europe 2025. That pedigree gives Shire a hole already shown to finish the Vendée Globe under reduced foiling, which directly supports the Grade 2 solo-qualifier requirement and the top 37 Globe Series target. The 17th August update keeps the focus on preparation rather than new travel, which fits the next concrete step of route-to-rum qualification.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:43.080
Now the fleet. The inaugural Ocean Race Atlantic starts 1st September from New York with 6 IMOCA entries, each carrying a mixed crew of two women and two men, racing to Lorient with expected arrivals around 8-9 September. Teams include pairing such as Francesca Clapsich with Paul Mailhat and Boris Herman's Malaysia 4 on its racing debut.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:56.240
The event forms part of the IMOCA Globe Series and functions as crewed preparation ahead of larger solo and crewed objectives. The 50-slash-50 gender rule is written into the notice of race and checked at the dock.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:05.320
The race tests organization and sporting stakes across the six-boat fleet while serving as a live experiment in mixed crew operations on high performance boats.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:15.640
This format tests how mixed teams manage a fully crewed transatlantic on high performance boats, establishing data and experience that could shape future race rules and team selection.

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:29.120
Lewis Duke's new FOILA REACH CON described by the skipper as the concrete start of his 2028 Vendée Globe campaign. The arrival marks the physical beginning of Duke's program after the boats delivery and commissioning phase.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:38.580
New generation boats entering the water raise the performance baseline the rest of the fleet, including Emyra 4, Must Match or Exceed in the qualification window.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:48.060
Duke's statement ties the boats presence in CON directly to the launch of a full campaign cycle that will run through the Root Doohrum and into the Globe Series points battle.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:54.660
The move places another recent acquisition into active preparation alongside established hulls already racing in the class.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:03:00.380
Conrad Coleman will lead the first all-kewey IMOCA crew in the Ocean Race Atlantic.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:07.840
The crew announcement brings together New Zealand sailors for a fully national lineup in a class historically dominated by French programs.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:18.460
An all-national crew outside the traditional French Corps broadens the class's international visibility and brings distinct preparation and sponsorship dynamics into a high stakes crewed event.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:28.420
The team will race Malaysia for sister event alongside established pairings testing whether a single country roster can match the depth of experience found in mixed European squads.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:36.200
The debut gives Coleman a platform to demonstrate crew cohesion on a boat making its own racing return. That brings us to plain sailing.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:44.160
The ocean race Atlantic requires every boat to carry exactly two women and two men. That 50 slash 50 rule is not a suggestion.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:59.900
It is written into the notice of race and checked at the dock before the start. In practice, it forces teams to build four person watches that balance strength, weight, technical skill and decision making across gender lines on a boat that still demands the same physical loads as any IMOCA.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:07.240
Grinding winches, handling 40 knot squals and executing sail changes while the hull is flying on one or two foils.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:16.440
Because the race is crude rather than solo, the skippers roll shifts from sole decision maker to coordinator who must integrate four sets of observations and fatigue patterns.

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:23.460
On a solo boat, the skipper alone reads the weather, chooses the sail plan and decides when to push or conserve.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:30.380
Here those tasks are distributed, which changes both the speed of decisions and the risk of conflicting inputs at three in the morning.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:34.640
The format therefore tests both the human system and the technical system.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:44.580
Teams, such as the Clapsich Mail Hat Pairing and the All QE crew under Coleman, will reveal whether national or cross-border combinations handle that coordination differently.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:58.920
The first edition therefore functions as a live experiment. Results will show whether the gender balance rule produces measurably different speeds, safety records or breakage rates compared with the all-male or open crews that dominate most IMOCA events.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:01.100
Before we go, the countdown.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:06.520
804 days remain until the Vande Globe start on November 12th, 2028.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:13.480
The campaign's next concrete milestone is the Root Do Ram, starting November 1st, 2026 from St. Milo.

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:15.720
And that's the week in offshore racing.

